-- Q11802: Income Taxes [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 740-10-45-4, a deferred tax benefit reduces total income tax expense and arises when DTLs decrease or DTAs increase during the period. It represents anticipated future tax savings recognized on an accrual basis.
Wrong (B): A tax refund is a cash receipt from the taxing authority related to overpayment, not a deferred tax benefit. Deferred tax benefits are accrual-basis adjustments.
Wrong (C): An NOL carryback generates a current tax benefit, not a deferred tax benefit. Deferred tax benefits relate to changes in DTAs and DTLs, not current refund claims.
Wrong (D): Cash tax savings occur when temporary differences actually reverse. A deferred tax benefit is the accrual-basis recognition, which may occur in a different period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11802;

-- Q11803: Income Taxes [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 740-20-45-2, intraperiod tax allocation requires that the tax effect of OCI items be allocated directly to those items. Each OCI component is presented net of its own tax effect rather than flowing through income tax expense.
Wrong (A): Combining OCI tax effects with continuing operations tax expense would distort both the income statement and the OCI presentation. Intraperiod allocation prevents this.
Wrong (B): The tax effect of OCI is not a separate income statement line item. It is allocated within OCI itself, not reported on the income statement.
Wrong (C): Tax effects of OCI items are recognized when the OCI items are recognized, not deferred until reclassified to earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11803;

-- Q11804: Income Taxes [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 740-10-30-5, changes in the valuation allowance are recognized in income tax expense from continuing operations. Reducing the allowance from $150,000 to $40,000 releases $110,000, decreasing income tax expense by that amount.
Wrong (A): An increase of $110,000 reverses the direction. Releasing a valuation allowance decreases, not increases, income tax expense because it reflects greater expected DTA realization.
Wrong (B): A decrease of $40,000 confuses the remaining allowance balance with the change. The change is $150,000 minus $40,000 = $110,000.
Wrong (D): A decrease of $150,000 incorrectly eliminates the entire original allowance. Management still requires $40,000, so only $110,000 is released.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11804;

-- Q11805: Income Taxes [medium/L2]
